## General Rules (all languages)
- Security: SQL injection, XSS, hardcoded secrets/credentials, missing input validation, sensitive data exposed in logs.
- Error handling: swallowed exceptions (empty catch), missing logging at critical points, generic error messages to end users.
- Readability: methods too long or with too many responsibilities (SRP), poor naming, dead/commented-out code.
- Duplication: repeated logic that should be extracted/reused.
- Unused code: unused local variables, method parameters, private fields/methods, unreachable code, and unused imports/usings.
- Mandatory language consistency: Newly added or modified identifiers, developer-facing comments, and documentation must use the same natural language already established by the surrounding code and project. Report mixed-language naming or documentation as a `style` finding on the identifier declaration or comment line. For example, a Portuguese method name in a codebase whose existing identifiers are English is a violation. Do not flag names imposed by external APIs, standard technical terms, or localized user-facing text.

<!-- lang: cs -->
## C#
- Mandatory naming conventions: Every newly added or modified C# identifier must follow Microsoft's official naming guidance. Report each violation as a `style` finding on the    identifier's declaration line. Use PascalCase for types, namespaces, all methods and local functions, public or protected members, and constants; prefix interfaces with `I`; use camelCase for parameters and local variables; use `_camelCase` for private or internal instance fields and `s_camelCase` for private or internal static fields. Names must be meaningful and descriptive, and nonstandard abbreviations must be avoided. Reference: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/coding-style/identifier-names
- Mandatory XML documentation: Every newly added or modified public or protected C# type and member must have valid XML documentation comments.
Report missing documentation as a `style` finding on the declaration line. Require `<summary>` and, when applicable, `<param>`, `<typeparam>` and
`<returns>` elements. Follow Microsoft's XML documentation guidance: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/xmldoc/
- Mandatory null safety: Report a `bug` whenever newly added or modified
code dereferences an externally supplied reference-type parameter without first validating it for null, unless non-nullability is explicitly
established by preceding code. Recommend `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(parameter)` and report the finding on the first unsafe dereference.
- Async: avoid `async void` (except handlers), use `ConfigureAwait(false)` in libraries, don't block with `.Result`/`.Wait()`.
- Nullable refs: correct `?` usage, avoid unjustified `!`.
- Immutability: prefer `readonly`, records, immutable types.
